---

<Base title="28. Attributes, decorators, reflection — C# vs TypeScript" description="28. Attributes, decorators, reflection: side-by-side C# and TypeScript examples." slug="attributes">
  <div slot="header" id="top">
    <p><small><a href="/">C# vs TS</a> / 28. Attributes, decorators, reflection</small></p>
    <h1>28. Attributes, decorators, reflection</h1>
    <p><strong>How to read:</strong> left is C#, middle is TypeScript, right is blank Mystery (yours). Narrow screens stack.</p>
  </div>

  <section id="attributes">
  
  <h2>28. Attributes, decorators, reflection</h2>
  <Compare>
    <div slot="left">
      <pre><code>[Obsolete("Use NewMethod instead")]
public void OldMethod() &#123; /* ... */ &#125;

[AttributeUsage(AttributeTargets.Class)]
public class RouteAttribute(string path) : Attribute
&#123; public string Path =&gt; path; &#125;

[Route("/users")]
public class UsersController &#123; &#125;

var attr = typeof(UsersController)
  .GetCustomAttributes(typeof(RouteAttribute), false)
  .Cast&lt;RouteAttribute&gt;().First();

// source generators: compile-time codegen, AOT-safe</code></pre>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <pre><code>/** @deprecated Use newMethod instead. */
export function oldMethod() &#123;&#125;

// TC39 decorators (TS 5+)
function Route(path: string) &#123;
  return function (target: Function) &#123;
    Reflect.defineMetadata?.("route", path, target);
  &#125;;
&#125;
@Route("/users")
export class UsersController &#123; &#125;

// reflection ≈ manual metadata + schemas
// runtime type info is NOT automatic like C#
// prefer Zod/Valibot over decorator magic for validation</code></pre>
    </div>
  </Compare>
  <ul>
    <li>C# attributes + reflection are runtime-first (ASP.NET, serializers). TS decorators are syntax sugar — runtime types need explicit libraries.</li>
  </ul>

  </section>

